        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.648,
            0.047,
            0.845,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Working with Fractions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.584,
            0.233
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Now, we need to multiply $\\frac { 2 } { 2 0 }$ by 3. Distance covered in $\\frac { 3 } { 4 }$ of an hour $= 3 \\times { \\frac { 2 } { 2 0 } }$ $= { \\frac { 6 } { 2 0 } }$ So, $\\frac{3}{4} \\times \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{6}{20} = \\frac{3}{10}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.647,
            0.084,
            0.824,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.252,
            0.288,
            0.272
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Discussion"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.283,
            0.542,
            0.377
          ],
          "content": "In the case of a fraction multiplied by another fraction, we follow a method similar to the one we used, when we multiplied a fraction by a whole number. We multiplied as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.568,
            0.299,
            0.648,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": "Multiplier"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.551,
            0.284,
            0.848,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.729,
            0.298,
            0.828,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": "Multiplicand"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.241,
            0.388,
            0.755,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": "4 $\\frac { 2 } { 5 } \\div 4 = \\frac { 2 } { 2 0 }$ Divide the multiplicand by 4. 34 $\\frac{3}{3} \\times \\frac{2}{20} = \\frac{6}{20} =$ Divide the multiplicand by 3."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.507,
            0.556,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": "Using this understanding, multiply ${ \\frac { 5 } { 4 } } \\times { \\frac { 3 } { 2 } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.494,
            0.903,
            0.547
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.547,
            0.655,
            0.63
          ],
          "content": "First, let us represent $\\frac{3}{2},$ taking the unit square as the whole. Since, the fraction $\\frac { 3 } { 2 }$ is one whole and a half, it can be seen as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.666,
            0.571,
            0.846,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.648,
            0.655,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "Following the steps of multiplication, we need to first divide this fraction $\\frac{3}{2}$ into 4 equal parts. It can be done as shown in the Fig. 8.2 with the yellow shaded region representing the fraction obtained by dividing $\\frac { 3 } { 2 }$ into 4 equal parts. What is its value?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.718,
            0.739,
            0.781,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 8.2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.79,
            0.569,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": "We see that the whole is divided into — 2 rows and 4 columns, creating $2 \\times 4 = 8$ equal parts. Number of parts shaded = 3. So the yellow shaded part $= { \\frac { 3 } { 8 } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
